#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:9
msgid "NetBSD support"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:11
msgid ""
"Roughly two months have passed since I last :doc:`announced </blog/2015/"
"openbsd-support>` that psutil added support for OpenBSD. Today I'm happy to "
"announce that it's the turn of NetBSD! This was contributed by `Thomas "
"Klausner <https://github.com/0-wiz-0>`_, `Ryo Onodera <https://github.com/"
"ryoon>`_ and myself in :pr:`557`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:18
msgid "Differences with FreeBSD (and OpenBSD)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:20
msgid ""
"The NetBSD implementation has limitations similar to the ones I encountered "
"with OpenBSD. Again, FreeBSD remains the BSD variant with the best support "
"in terms of kernel functionality."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:24
msgid ""
":meth:`Process.memory_maps` is not implemented. The kernel provides the "
"necessary pieces, but I haven't done this yet (hopefully later)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:26
msgid ""
":meth:`Process.num_ctx_switches`'s :field:`involuntary` field is always 0. "
"The ``kinfo_proc()`` syscall provides this info, but it's always set to 0."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:28
msgid ":meth:`Process.cpu_affinity` (get and set) is not supported."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:29
msgid ":func:`psutil.cpu_count` ``(logical=False)`` always returns ``None``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:31
msgid ""
"As for the rest: it is all there. All memory, disk, network and process APIs "
"are fully supported and functioning."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:35
msgid "Other enhancements available in this psutil release"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:37
msgid ""
"Besides NetBSD support, this release has a couple of interesting "
"enhancements:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:39
msgid ""
":gh:`708`: [Linux] :func:`psutil.net_connections` and :meth:`Process."
"connections` can be up to 3x faster when there are many connections."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:42
msgid ":gh:`718`: :func:`psutil.process_iter` is now thread safe."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../blog/2016/netbsd-support.rst:44
msgid "You can read the rest in the :ref:`changelog <341>`, as usual."
msgstr ""

